        <noteGrp type="abstract">
          <desc xml:lang="en">Abstract</desc>
          <note corresp="http://syriaca.org/nhsl" xml:lang="en" type="abstract" source="#bib5745-4 #bib5745-10 #bib5745-11">A doxographical survey of ancient views on the first principles of the universe, forming the introduction to the second base of <persName ref="http://syriaca.org/person/239">Bar Hebraeus</persName>'s <title>Candelabrum of the Sanctuary</title>. Its account of the Greek philosophers, from Thales to Atticus and other interpreters of Plato's <title>Timaeus</title>, draws largely on a Syriac treatise identified as a version of <persName ref="http://syriaca.org/person/684">Porphyry</persName>'s lost <title>On Principles and Matter</title>; Bar Hebraeus added <persName ref="http://syriaca.org/person/3">Bardaisan</persName> and <persName ref="http://syriaca.org/person/608">Mani</persName>. He sets out these views only to reject them together, since all of them assume an eternal, uncreated world.</note>
        </noteGrp>
